      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the incidence of complications, including recanalization, in a series of 6248 consecutive vasectomies performed with a section-fulguration-fascial interposition technique."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Over a 38-year period, 6248 vasectomies were performed by one surgeon (S.S.S.) as a clinic procedure under local anaesthesia with no resection of a vasal segment. The mucosa of the cut ends of the vas was destroyed by cauterization and the fascial sheath of the vas was interposed as a barrier. Semen specimens were examined until two specimens, one month apart, showed no sperm."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Complications were minimal, with few cases of haematoma or wound infection. Spermatic granulomas were uncommon. No post-vasectomy pregnancies were reported and no patient showed a persistence of sperm."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The section-fulguration-fascial interposition technique of vasectomy was uniformly effective, with few post-operative problems."
        }
      ]},
